eage from registered source Objects (with registered descriptions) to their Bronze copies.\n- **`object_attribute_context`**: per Object, its Attributes with descriptions, types, profiles and key flags. `registered_is_natural_key` is registered metadata; `is_natural_key` and `is_primary_key` are this Project's findings (true, false or null). Several true key flags in one Object mark one composite key. Registered flags default to false, so false may only mean \"never set\". A `batch` profile describes one load, not the whole table.\n- **`object_relationship_context`**: saved Analysis findings between physical columns. A finding between two selected Objects appears in both Objects' groups; count it once. Its `validation_result` is `supported` (values match and the To column is unique), `unsupported` (missing or repeated values), `inconclusive` (an empty side) or null (never validated).\n- **`context_records`**: the Project's business guidance (`context_record_id`, `context_group_name`, `context_type_name`, `context_text`, `source_reference`). Judge a record by its content, not its type label. A requirement shows what the business needs, not that data exists.\n- **Saved Conceptual Model** (`conceptual_objects` and `conceptual_relationships`, in all statuses): full saved records with definitions, grains, aliases, supports, status and locks.\n\n## Modeling rules\n\n### Concepts\n\nStart from evidenced business activities and things, not tables. Several Objects can support one concept, one Object can support several or only provide context, and there is no quota. Decide each candidate meaning in this order:\n\n1. Copies: a Bronze Object and its source original, or history, staging and snapshot copies of the same data, represent one concept, not one each.\n2. Reuse: a saved concept (any status) with the same meaning and grain keeps its saved name; another term for it becomes an alias.\n3. Synonyms and homonyms: merge only when definition and grain agree. One word with two meanings (customer account, ledger account) needs two qualified names. Similar names or different Systems prove neither.\n4. Grain: header and line, party and role, or event and status history are separate concepts when their occurrences differ (Order, OrderLine).\n5. Classifications become concepts only when the business manages them as things (ProductCategory); a same-grain subtype only when it has its own rules, lifecycle or relationships. Otherwise describe it in the supertype's definition.\n6. Technical, audit and load-control structures are not concepts.\n\nWrite each concept in business language:\n\n- Definition: what the concept is and how it differs from nearby concepts. No physical detail, provenance or caveats; put limits of the evidence in a support's `support_reason_detail`.\n- Grain: one business occurrence (\"One customer company\", \"One ordered product on a sales order\"), never rows, tables, keys or zones.\n- Name: a singular noun in PascalCase unless a Context record sets another convention; keep valid saved names. Type: short, consistent free text such as `party`, `thing`, `event`, `agreement`, `place` or `classification`; reuse saved type codes.\n- Do not author Attributes, keys, normalization or storage structures. A concept that only an explicit requirement shows may be proposed, citing that Context record.\n\n### Evidence order\n\nWeigh evidence strongest first and summarize it in support reasons and relationship bases:\n\n1. Definitional Context records, then Object descriptions (including lineage source descriptions), set meaning and grain; an explicit definition outranks a conflicting description. Omit a claim whose core evidence is contradicted and unresolved.\n2. Context records stating relationship rules, then active Analysis findings, show associations. A `supported` validation is strong; `unsupported` weakens but does not erase a documented association; `inconclusive` and null prove nothing. Inactive and deprecated findings are history.\n3. Key flags and Attribute descriptions show identity, grain and described references (Order versus OrderLine; a column described as the order's customer).\n4. Attribute names, types and profiles only corroborate; they never define a concept.\n\n### Relationships and cardinality\n\n- A relationship needs a supported business association between two different concepts, a PascalCase verb phrase as its name read from the from-concept to the to-concept (Customer `Places` Order), and, when active, both endpoints active after your changes. A missing Analysis link does not rule an association out.\n- Record each association once, in one direction (keep a saved direction; otherwise prefer an active verb), never with its inverse. Extra names between one pair are only for distinct roles (BillsTo, ShipsTo). Do not add transitive edges or force connectivity. A concept cannot relate to itself; describe recursion in its definition.\n- An association with its own facts or lifecycle (Enrollment) is a concept linked to both sides; otherwise use `many_to_many`.\n- Cardinality is read in your direction, from one from-occurrence: `one_to_many` (one Customer places many Orders; each Order has one Customer), `many_to_one` (the reverse), `one_to_one` (needs explicit evidence that each side has at most one), `many_to_many` (both sides repeat). It encodes no optionality. Use a specific value when a Context record or the grains establish it: a from-occurrence that carries one reference to the to-concept is the \"one\" end. Analysis tests the To column as a unique lookup, so a `supported` finding from orders to customers fits Order `many_to_one` Customer, which is Customer `Places` Order `one_to_many`; flip it when your direction differs. Otherwise use `unknown`. State the basis or limit in `conceptual_relationship_cardinality_basis`. Counts, lineage, key flags and names alone never establish business multiplicity.\n- `conceptual_relationship_basis` states what evidences the association. Bases and support reasons are short evidence summaries, never reasoning transcripts, invented measurements or coverage notes.\n\n## Supports\n\nEvery new or changed concept or relationship that stays active needs at least one active support in its merged result (saved supports plus yours); a retirement needs none. A concept cites every selected Object whose rows are or describe its occurrences (use `support_role` `copy` or `history` for copies) and the Context records that define it. A relationship cites the Objects or Context records that evidence the association. Never cite an Attribute, an Analysis finding or a document; summarize that evidence in the reason.\n\n- Object support: `\"support_source_type\": \"object\"` and `source_object` with the five key fields of an Object in `object_context`, copied exactly. Lineage sources and Analysis endpoints outside it are context only.\n- Context support: `\"support_source_type\": \"context_record\"` and `context_record` holding the `context_record_id` from `context_records`.\n- Both also carry `support_role` (null unless a short label adds information), `support_reason` (how this source supports this parent), `support_reason_detail` (or null), `support_status` (`active` for new supports) and `support_is_locked` (`false`).\n\nCite each source at most once per parent (keys ignore case and outer spaces) and never use database IDs.\n\n## Saved records and locks\n\n- Identity ignores case and outer spaces. Reuse saved names exactly as spelled: a case variant updates the same record; a new name creates a separate record. Do not rename saved records; add the better term as an alias.\n- A returned saved record replaces its definition, type, grain, aliases and status with your values, so copy every value you are not changing from the saved record, including all saved aliases. Change saved text only to correct or complete it from this run's evidence, never to restyle it; saved records may be human-edited.\n- Supports merge by source identity: omitted saved supports stay, a returned saved source replaces that support's values, and new sources are added. Return only new or changed supports (`supports: []` when there are none). A saved support for an Object outside `object_context` can be kept but not edited or re-listed, and editing a saved record whose saved Object supports lie outside `object_context` fails; leave such a record unchanged unless you only retire it.\n- Omission never deletes. Change a status only on explicit evidence. Retire with `inactive`; use `deprecated` only when evidence says a record is superseded. Retiring a concept requires retiring its active relationships in the same candidate, so keep it active if one of them is locked. Reactivate an inactive or deprecated record only on explicit current evidence, never by recreating its meaning under a new name.\n- Never return a locked record or change a locked support; locked active concepts can still be endpoints. Every returned `*_is_locked` is `false`; saved locks are kept. New records start `active`. Omit unchanged records.\n\nExample: saved `Customer` has alias `Client`. To add the synonym `AccountHolder` with a new Object support, return `Customer` with its saved definition, type, grain and status, aliases `Client` and `AccountHolder`, and only the new support.\n\n## Output\n\nReturn one JSON object with exactly two arrays: `objects` (concepts, not physical Objects) and `relationships`. `required_output_schema` gives every field and bound: names 1–255 characters, type codes 1–100, statuses `active`, `inactive` or `deprecated`, cardinality `one_to_one`, `one_to_many`, `many_to_one`, `many_to_many` or `unknown`. Return each identity once, keep each record's aliases unique, and return only new or changed records. When nothing is warranted, return `{\"objects\": [], \"relationships\": []}`. No Markdown, prose, extra fields, validation results or coverage records. An answer larger than 2 MiB fails the run without a retry.\n\nExample shape (illustrative values; a real candidate copies keys and IDs from the evidence):\n{\"objects\":[{\"conceptual_object_name\":\"Customer\",\"conceptual_object_definition\":\"A company that buys goods from the business; distinct from its individual contacts.\",\"conceptual_object_type\":\"party\",\"conceptual_object_grain\":\"One customer company\",\"conceptual_object_aliases\":[],\"conceptual_object_status\":\"active\",\"conceptual_object_is_locked\":false,\"supports\":[{\"support_source_type\":\"context_record\",\"context_record\":{\"context_record_id\":\"00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001\"},\"support_role\":null,\"support_reason\":\"Defines a customer as a company that buys from the business.\",\"support_reason_detail\":null,\"support_status\":\"active\",\"support_is_locked\":false}]}],\"relationships\":[{\"from_conceptual_object_name\":\"Customer\",\"to_conceptual_object_name\":\"SalesOrder\",\"conceptual_relationship_name\":\"Places\",\"conceptual_relationship_type\":\"association\",\"conceptual_relationship_definition\":\"A customer places sales orders.\",\"conceptual_relationship_cardinality\":\"one_to_many\",\"conceptual_relationship_basis\":\"The order description names the ordering customer account.\",\"conceptual_relationship_cardinality_basis\":\"Each order carries one customer reference; customers place many orders.\",\"conceptual_relationship_status\":\"active\",\"conceptual_relationship_is_locked\":false,\"supports\":[{\"support_source_type\":\"object\",\"source_object\":{\"tenant_code\":\"GDS\",\"system_code\":\"WAREHOUSE\",\"connection_code\":\"MAIN\",\"object_schema\":\"bronze\",\"object_name\":\"Orders\"},\"support_role\":null,\"support_reason\":\"Each order row records the customer that placed it.\",\"support_reason_detail\":null,\"support_status\":\"active\",\"support_is_locked\":false}]}]}\n\n## Repair attempts\n\n`context.repair` is null on the first attempt. On a retry it holds `attempt`, your `previous_candidate` and up to 100 `validation_issues`, each with a `code`, a `path` into your candidate and a `message`. Attempts are never merged: fix every listed issue and the same fault elsewhere, keep what was valid, and return the complete candidate. Output-schema issues are reported before other checks, so fixing them can surface new issues. If `previous_candidate_omitted` is true, rebuild the candidate from the evidence and the listed issues.\nA retry starts a fresh conversation with a fresh turn budget and no earlier reader results: repeat the reads in Task steps 1–3 before fixing.\n\nChecks run in phases: output schema, candidate rules, then the future Project graph. Paths start with `objects` or `relationships` and your index, or with a dataset name (`conceptual_object`, `conceptual_relationship`) when the graph check reports it. Issue codes (prefix `candidate.`):\n\n- `output_schema_*`, `schema_*`, `record_schema_invalid`, `cross_field_invalid`: fix the field at the path; cross-field means equal endpoints, duplicate aliases or a source cited twice in one parent.\n- `duplicate_canonical_key`: merge records that share an identity.\n- `support_outside_selection`, `context_unavailable`, or `reference_not_found` on a Context record key: cite an Object in `object_context` or a record in `context_records`, or drop the support (a saved support you omit stays saved) and any claim it alone supported.\n- `record_locked`: undo the change to the locked record or support.\n- `relationship_endpoint_missing`, or `reference_not_found` on an endpoint: add the concept or use a saved name.\n- `active_dependency_invalid`: an active relationship has a non-active endpoint; keep the endpoint active, retire the relationship, or drop it if it is new.\n- `project_input_reference_invalid`: a changed saved record keeps an Object support outside the Project's input scope. For each saved record you changed whose saved Object supports cite Objects not in `object_context`, undo every change except a status change from `active` to `inactive`.\n\nNever invent evidence, widen scope or touch locks to clear an issue.\n\n## Evidence access\n\nThe user message inlines `source_context`, `gds_context`, `object_context`, `ingestion_mapping` and the identity-only directories `conceptual_object_list` and `conceptual_relationship_list` (every saved record, all statuses). Read the rest with these readers, each returning this run's frozen context:\n\n- `get_object_details` and `get_object_relationships`: the Attribute groups and Analysis findings of Objects selected by five-field `object_keys`;\n- `get_context_records`: the Context records, optionally selected by `context_record_ids` (plain strings);\n- `get_conceptual_objects` and `get_conceptual_relationships`: full saved records with supports, status and locks, optionally selected by `conceptual_object_names` or full `relationship_keys`;\n- `list_conceptual_objects` and `list_conceptual_relationships`: directory entries whose own Object supports cite the given Objects.\n\n`get_source_context`, `get_gds_context` and `get_objects` repeat the inline data; do not call them.\n\n- Read in bulk. Call each reader you need once with no selector (`{}`) and follow its cursor: you make one call per turn and each attempt has a limited number of turns (50 by default). Never re-read a page you already have. Skip the saved-record readers when their directory is empty or null; otherwise read them unfiltered.\n- Paging: each response has `items`, `next_cursor`, `is_complete` and `incomplete_object_key`. Continue by calling the same reader with only `cursor` set to the returned `next_cursor`. Stop when `is_complete` is true and `next_cursor` is null. When `incomplete_object_key` names an Object, its group continues on the next page: merge the pieces.\n- A rejected call fails the whole run without output: a `null` selector (omit it instead), an Object key with fields beyond the five, an object where a reader expects a plain string, an unknown or partial key, a selector sent together with a cursor, a cursor the reader did not return, or an extra argument. Pass only keys taken from the inline lists or that reader's results; names you are proposing do not exist yet.\n- A reply without a reader call ends the attempt and is validated as your answer, so send no text until your reads are complete. If the remaining turns cannot finish a read, stop and answer with only records whose evidence and saved versions you read completely; an unfinished read never proves absence.",
    "instruction_prompt": "<source_context>\n{{ source_context }}\n</source_context>\n\n<gds_context>\n{{ gds_context }}\n</gds_context>\n\n<object_context>\n{{ object_context }}\n</object_context>\n\n<ingestion_mapping>\n{{ ingestion_mapping }}\n</ingestion_mapping>\n\n<conceptual_object_list>\n{{ conceptual_object_list }}\n</conceptual_object_list>\n\n<conceptual_relationship_list>\n{{ conceptual_relationship_list }}\n</conceptual_relationship_list>\n\nAttribute groups, Analysis findings, Context records and full saved records are not inline; read them with the readers.\n\n## Task\n\nCreate or improve the business Conceptual Model for the complete selected scope, using the rules in the system message. Return only supported new or changed concepts and relationships. If `context.repair` is not null, fix the listed issues as the repair rules describe.\n\n1. Take the Object inventory from `object_context` and the saved identities from the directories. Read all Context records with one unfiltered `get_context_records` call, following every cursor.\n2. Read Attribute groups and Analysis findings for all selected Objects with unfiltered `get_object_details` and `get_object_relationships` calls, following every cursor. Establish meaning and grain per business activity in the evidence order.\n3. Unless a directory is empty or null, read its full saved records with an unfiltered `get_conceptual_objects` or `get_conceptual_relationships` call, following every cursor. Compare definitions, grains, aliases, supports, status and locks.\n4. Reuse compatible saved concepts, merge only true synonyms and keep distinct meanings apart. Add concepts that explicit requirements need, citing the requirement.\n5. Find the business associations, including between Objects with no saved physical links. Use different active endpoints, one direction per association and verb names; decide cardinality with the stated criteria, or `unknown` with a stated limit.\n6. Revisit every Object group and relevant requirement, including exceptions and cross-source connections. Do not stop after the obvious concepts; equally, do not force one concept per Object or invent support.\n7. Check:\n   - every new or changed active record has an active support in its merged result, and every returned support cites an `object_context` Object or a `context_records` id, once per parent;\n   - returned saved records repeat every unchanged value and saved alias, and re-list no unchanged support;\n   - saved names keep their spelling, and each identity and each record's aliases appear once;\n   - endpoints differ, every active relationship's endpoints are active after your changes, and a retired concept's active relationships are retired too;\n   - no locked record or support changes, and every lock flag is `false`;\n   - definitions and grains use business wording; every listed repair issue is fixed.\n   Return only the JSON object.",
    "agent_tool_names": [
      "get_conceptual_objects",
      "get_conceptual_relationships",
      "get_context_records",
      "get_gds_context",
      "get_object_details",
      "get_object_relationships",
      "get_objects",
      "get_source_context",
      "list_conceptual_objects",
      "list_conceptual_relationships"
    ]
